The datum (Ã →σ S, σ_Ã) is a graph in Serre's sense, equivalently a graph given by half-edges:
- vertices are the components;
- oriented edges (half-edges) are the branches at nodes;
- σ is the origin map;
- σ_Ã is edge reversal;
- the extremity of a is σ(σ_Ã(a)).

This is the dual graph of the nodal curve. It depends only on \hat X, not on the choice of the marked points. To record the marking, Grothendieck adds the map (9) p: I → S, which attaches 'legs' (marked points) to vertices. The resulting object is today's marked or stable graph, which indexes the boundary strata of \bar M_{g,ν].

